<!--
    The app's light theme.

    A theme maps raw colours onto Material 3's *role* attributes. Every widget in the app asks for
    a role — ?attr/colorPrimary, ?attr/colorSurface — rather than a colour, so restyling the whole
    app means editing this one file. values-night/themes.xml declares the same roles with the dark
    values, which is the entire mechanism behind dark mode: no layout knows which is in use.

    The theme also sets the app's **default component styles** — materialCardViewStyle and the shape
    scale. That is what makes the redesign hold: a card added to a new screen next month is rounded,
    white and softly shadowed because the theme says every card is, not because whoever wrote it
    remembered to name a style.

    The parent is Theme.Material3.DayNight.NoActionBar. "NoActionBar" is required because the app
    supplies its own MaterialToolbar in activity_main.xml — with the platform action bar still in
    place there would be two title bars stacked on top of each other.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <style name="Base.Theme.PetCare" parent="Theme.Material3.DayNight.NoActionBar">

        <!-- Primary: the bright yellow accent. Carries the main actions — the FAB, filled buttons,
             the selected navigation tab. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/md_theme_light_primary</item>
        <item name="colorOnPrimary">@color/md_theme_light_onPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryContainer">@color/md_theme_light_primaryContainer</item>
        <item name="colorOnPrimaryContainer">@color/md_theme_light_onPrimaryContainer</item>

        <!-- Secondary: supporting elements that should not compete with the primary action. -->
        <item name="colorSecondary">@color/md_theme_light_secondary</item>
        <item name="colorOnSecondary">@color/md_theme_light_onSecondary</item>
        <item name="colorSecondaryContainer">@color/md_theme_light_secondaryContainer</item>
        <item name="colorOnSecondaryContainer">@color/md_theme_light_onSecondaryContainer</item>

        <!-- Tertiary: the contrasting accent, used sparingly. -->
        <item name="colorTertiary">@color/md_theme_light_tertiary</item>
        <item name="colorOnTertiary">@color/md_theme_light_onTertiary</item>
        <item name="colorTertiaryContainer">@color/md_theme_light_tertiaryContainer</item>
        <item name="colorOnTertiaryContainer">@color/md_theme_light_onTertiaryContainer</item>

        <!-- Error: destructive actions and validation messages. -->
        <item name="colorError">@color/md_theme_light_error</item>
        <item name="colorOnError">@color/md_theme_light_onError</item>
        <item name="colorErrorContainer">@color/md_theme_light_errorContainer</item>
        <item name="colorOnErrorContainer">@color/md_theme_light_onErrorContainer</item>

        <!-- Surfaces: the backgrounds content sits on, and the text colours guaranteed legible
             against them.

             colorBackground (cream) and colorSurface (white) are deliberately *different* values
             here. That one separation is the backbone of the redesign: it is what lets a white card
             read as lifted off the page before its shadow is even drawn. -->
        <item name="android:colorBackground">@color/md_theme_light_background</item>
        <item name="colorOnBackground">@color/md_theme_light_onBackground</item>
        <item name="colorSurface">@color/md_theme_light_surface</item>
        <item name="colorOnSurface">@color/md_theme_light_onSurface</item>
        <item name="colorSurfaceVariant">@color/md_theme_light_surfaceVariant</item>
        <item name="colorOnSurfaceVariant">@color/md_theme_light_onSurfaceVariant</item>

        <!-- Material 3 tints an elevated surface towards the primary colour to signal height — the
             "elevation overlay". With a yellow primary that turns every raised card faintly
             mustard, which fights the clean cream-and-white scheme. Switching it off leaves depth
             to be carried by the shadow alone, which is what the reference direction asks for.

             Safe to disable in *both* themes only because this app's palette already separates the
             page from the cards by colour — see colors.xml. In a scheme where background and
             surface were the same value, the overlay is the only thing making a raised card
             visible in dark mode, and turning it off would flatten the whole app. -->
        <item name="elevationOverlayEnabled">false</item>

        <!-- Outlines: text field borders and the stroke on outlined buttons. -->
        <item name="colorOutline">@color/md_theme_light_outline</item>
        <item name="colorOutlineVariant">@color/md_theme_light_outlineVariant</item>

        <!-- Inverse roles, used by Snackbars so they read as layered above the app. -->
        <item name="colorSurfaceInverse">@color/md_theme_light_inverseSurface</item>
        <item name="colorOnSurfaceInverse">@color/md_theme_light_inverseOnSurface</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryInverse">@color/md_theme_light_inversePrimary</item>

        <!-- The accent as a foreground colour. Deeper than colorPrimary so it is legible as text —
             see attrs.xml for why this role has to exist at all. -->
        <item name="colorAccentText">@color/accent_text_light</item>

        <!-- The "chosen" fill on the Tasks tab: near-black with yellow on it in this theme. See
             attrs.xml. -->
        <item name="colorSelectedContainer">@color/selected_container_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnSelectedContainer">@color/on_selected_container_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnSelectedContainerNeutral">@color/on_selected_container_neutral_light</item>
        <!-- The same fill at rest, for a main button that cannot be pressed yet. See attrs.xml. -->
        <item name="colorDisabledContainer">@color/disabled_container_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnDisabledContainer">@color/on_disabled_container_light</item>

        <!-- ======================== Pastel stat pill roles ========================
             The app's own colour roles, declared in attrs.xml. See the note there for why a stat
             pill needs a family of *equal* colours that the Material roles cannot express. -->
        <item name="colorPillMint">@color/pill_light_mint</item>
        <item name="colorOnPillMint">@color/pill_light_on_mint</item>
        <item name="colorPillSky">@color/pill_light_sky</item>
        <item name="colorOnPillSky">@color/pill_light_on_sky</item>
        <item name="colorPillLilac">@color/pill_light_lilac</item>
        <item name="colorOnPillLilac">@color/pill_light_on_lilac</item>
        <item name="colorPillPeach">@color/pill_light_peach</item>
        <item name="colorOnPillPeach">@color/pill_light_on_peach</item>
        <!-- The profile screen's "VERIFIED" pill. Pale green in light mode; see attrs.xml. -->
        <item name="colorPillVerified">@color/verified_pill_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnPillVerified">@color/on_verified_pill_light</item>

        <!-- The log in screen's wrong-password warning cards — see attrs.xml. Pale washes of the
             accent and the error hue on the cream page. -->
        <item name="colorAuthAlertWarning">@color/auth_alert_warning_fill_light</item>
        <item name="colorAuthAlertWarningEdge">@color/auth_alert_warning_edge_light</item>
        <item name="colorAuthAlertDanger">@color/auth_alert_danger_fill_light</item>
        <item name="colorAuthAlertDangerEdge">@color/auth_alert_danger_edge_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnAuthAlertDanger">@color/auth_alert_danger_title_light</item>

        <!-- The floating navigation bar — see attrs.xml for why it needs roles of its own, and the
             bottom-navigation section of colors.xml for the values. In this theme the slab is white
             at 86% over the cream page and the active tab is a near-black pill with the accent
             written on it: the strongest mark available on a light page. -->
        <item name="colorNavBarSurface">@color/nav_bar_surface_light</item>
        <item name="colorNavTabActive">@color/nav_tab_active_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnNavTabActive">@color/on_nav_tab_active_light</item>
        <item name="colorNavTabMuted">@color/nav_tab_muted_light</item>
        <item name="colorNavBadgeActive">@color/nav_badge_active_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnNavBadgeActive">@color/on_nav_badge_active_light</item>

        <!-- The ground a map card shows while its map loads — see attrs.xml. In this theme a pale
             sage, which is the colour map paper reads as before any tiles are on it. -->
        <item name="colorMapGround">@color/map_ground_light</item>

        <!-- The vaccination status pills — see attrs.xml. Pale washes with dark ink in this theme;
             the night theme inverts that relationship for the first two. -->
        <item name="colorVaccinationClear">@color/vaccination_clear_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnVaccinationClear">@color/on_vaccination_clear_light</item>
        <item name="colorVaccinationOverdue">@color/vaccination_overdue_light</item>
        <item name="colorOnVaccinationOverdue">@color/on_vaccination_overdue_light</item>
        <item name="colorVaccinationDue">@color/vaccination_due</item>
        <item name="colorOnVaccinationDue">@color/on_vaccination_due</item>

        <!-- ============================== Shape scale ==============================
             Material components read their corner radius from these three attributes. Overriding
             them here rounds *everything* — dialogs, menus, text fields, bottom sheets — in one
             move, rather than each layout setting a radius the next screen forgets to match. -->
        <item name="shapeAppearanceCornerSmall">@style/ShapeAppearance.PetCare.Small</item>
        <item name="shapeAppearanceCornerMedium">@style/ShapeAppearance.PetCare.Medium</item>
        <item name="shapeAppearanceCornerLarge">@style/ShapeAppearance.PetCare.Large</item>

        <!-- ================================ Type scale ================================
             Material components ask the theme for a text *role* rather than naming a font, so
             re-pointing the fifteen roles at the styles in type.xml changes the typeface of every
             button, chip, dialog and text field in one place. See type.xml for which typeface plays
             which role and why. -->
        <item name="textAppearanceDisplayLarge">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.DisplayLarge</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceDisplayMedium">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.DisplayMedium</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceDisplaySmall">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.DisplaySmall</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceHeadlineLarge">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.HeadlineLarge</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceHeadlineMedium">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.HeadlineMedium</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceHeadlineSmall">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.HeadlineSmall</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceTitleLarge">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.TitleLarge</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceTitleMedium">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.TitleMedium</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceTitleSmall">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.TitleSmall</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceBodyLarge">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.BodyLarge</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceBodyMedium">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.BodyMedium</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceBodySmall">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.BodySmall</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceLabelLarge">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.LabelLarge</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceLabelMedium">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.LabelMedium</item>
        <item name="textAppearanceLabelSmall">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.LabelSmall</item>
        <!-- The fallback for a TextView with no text appearance of its own. -->
        <item name="android:textAppearance">@style/TextAppearance.PetCare.Default</item>

        <!-- ========================== Default component styles ==========================
             Pointing these theme attributes at the app's own styles is what applies the redesign
             to every instance of a component without a single layout naming a style. -->
        <!-- The bottom bar has no entry here. It is a MaterialCardView naming PetCare.NavBar
             explicitly in activity_main.xml, because it is one view rather than a component class
             the app uses repeatedly — pointing materialCardViewStyle at it would restyle every card
             in the app. -->
        <item name="materialCardViewStyle">@style/PetCare.Card</item>

        <!-- The status and navigation bar icons. The app's page is a light cream in this theme, so
             the system icons drawn over it must be dark to be visible at all. The night theme sets
             both to false for the same reason in reverse. -->
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar" tools:targetApi="o_mr1">true</item>
    </style>

    <style name="Theme.PetCare" parent="Base.Theme.PetCare" />

    <!--
        The theme MainActivity is *launched* with, and wears for a fraction of a second only.

        **What problem it solves.** Between the launcher icon being tapped and the app having any
        code running at all, the system draws a placeholder window filled with the theme's
        `android:windowBackground`. The app cannot influence that moment by any other means: there is
        no process yet to influence it with. Left to the ordinary theme, that background resolves to
        `?android:colorBackground` — the cream page in the light theme, which is fine, but the
        near-black page in the dark theme, which is the black flash the opening animation then has to
        replace. Pinning it to the animation's own ground here means the window opens in the same
        cream the overlay holds, so the handover is invisible in both themes.

        **Why it is a separate theme rather than one more item on Theme.PetCare.** windowBackground
        belongs to the window for the app's whole life, not just its first frame. Setting cream there
        would put a cream ground behind every screen in the dark theme — visible under every fragment
        transition, which animates one screen out before the next is opaque. So the Activity is
        launched with this and swaps to the real theme in onCreate, which is the standard Android
        pattern for exactly this; see MainActivity.onCreate.

        **The bar icons.** Inherited from Theme.PetCare, those follow the *theme*, so in the dark
        theme they would be light icons drawn over this cream ground — invisible. Forced dark here for
        the same reason the light theme forces them dark: what they are drawn on is light. The
        Activity puts them back under the real theme's control when the animation ends.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.PetCare.Splash" parent="Theme.PetCare">
        <!-- Up to Android 11, the placeholder window is filled with windowBackground. -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/splash_ground</item>

        <!-- From Android 12 it is not. The platform draws its own launch screen — the app icon on a
             ground of its own — and takes that ground from this attribute alone, falling back to
             ?android:colorBackground and ignoring windowBackground entirely. So the line above,
             correct as it is, has no effect whatsoever on a modern phone, and without this one the
             app opens on the near-black of the dark theme before the animation has a frame to show.
             **This was found by running it, not by reading it**: the launch window was still black on
             a device in dark mode with windowBackground set and apparently correct.

             Both lines are kept because they cover different versions, and minSdk is 24. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground" tools:targetApi="s">@color/splash_ground</item>

        <!-- And the one that actually did it on the device this was tested on.

             Neither line above changed anything there: the launch window kept coming up near-black in
             the dark theme with both set and both verified present in the built resources. What it
             was really reading is colorBackground — the attribute the other two are *documented* to
             override. Vendor launchers substitute their own starting-window implementation and this
             one evidently resolves the ground that way.

             So all three are set to the same colour. That is not redundancy for its own sake: the
             three are read by different Android versions and different vendors' launchers, none of
             them can be relied on alone, and agreeing on one value is what makes the launch cream
             wherever the app is installed rather than only where it was tested.

             Safe to set here precisely because this theme's life is over by the end of onCreate.
             colorBackground is the app's page colour and half the UI is drawn against it — but
             MainActivity swaps to Theme.PetCare before any of that UI is inflated, so nothing in the
             app ever reads this value. -->
        <item name="android:colorBackground">@color/splash_ground</item>

        <!-- **What this theme does and does not achieve, as measured rather than assumed.**

             All three lines above are genuinely honoured. That was established by temporarily setting
             splash_ground to magenta and relaunching: the launch window came up magenta, in the light
             theme and the dark theme alike. So the app's declared launch colour does reach the
             window.

             With the *cream* back, though, the launch window is cream only while the system is in its
             light theme. In dark mode the device repaints it to a near-black of its own choosing
             (#201E18 — not this colour, and not the app's own dark page colour either). The magenta
             run showed why: that came back slightly darkened too (#DF00E1), which is the signature of
             the platform's automatic dark treatment — it barely touches a saturated colour and
             inverts a light neutral like cream almost completely.

             **It cannot be declined from here.** android:forceDarkAllowed="false" is the documented
             opt-out and was tried: it changed nothing, because it governs the *app's* view hierarchy,
             and this window is drawn by the system before the app has one. So on a device whose
             system theme is dark there is still a brief dark frame before the animation appears. The
             app's own theme defaults to following the system, so the two agree — what the user sees
             is a dark frame, then the animation, then a dark app.

             Left as it is deliberately: it is correct wherever the platform allows it to be, and the
             remaining case is a vendor behaviour, not a missing line here. -->

        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar" tools:targetApi="o_mr1">true</item>
    </style>

</resources>
